tructural limitations or  some of the limitations that that are  built into the system that need to be  overcome or explored to allow some of  the direction to go the other way we've  you know scientists we often have to  reach out to other scientists at Yale  who who have cooler stuff than we do and  and better skills and we routinely see a  high level of enthusiasm and and often  followed up by actual work but what we  know that that we have to sort of limit  ourselves in terms of what we can ask to  is this going to be an imposition on  someone's research agenda and how that  impacts their ability to support their  postdocs and graduate students and and  their promotion in our field yes I'd be  interested in your views as the  equipment becomes more exciting and  sophisticated to be able to identify the  history of an artistic piece that you  look at in preservation and conservation  if indeed that makes it easier or more  difficult philosophically on how far you  go to preserve an an object for example  the medieval piece that you're working  on you who said definitely needs work  how far that seems to always be the  issue is how far do you go when you know  so much that's  behind it all well there's one systems I  didn t have time to mention which is  called reflectance transform imaging  which enlarge you to give a very very  detailed topography of the surface and  when this is first begun to be used in  conservation to examine for example the  surface of paintings some of us thought  this was actually a tool to prevent any  further conservation work being done  because if you're physically trying to  remove some later addition to a painting  the mud slightest kind of scratch on the  surface you might make the scalpel in  theory would show up in RTI but I think  what happened is that that's actually  made us very much more aware of the  surface and when you look back at older  conservation records I mean people had a  very kind of cavalier attitude to how  things behaved and now i think these  tools help us to really understand not  only the physical materials but also  what's happened to them in their history  either through being exposure to sort of  hazardous treatments or being shown  stored in conditions that led to their  deterioration so that you you can see  that something that should be hard and  durable has become soft or compromised  by soft material being added to ities so  these were the tools are incredibly  valuable and I think will continue to be  developed and used an RTI also has the  amazing ability to show all of the folds  and creases in paper so it's abyss we've  actually been in contact with the RTI  people yeah and if anybody is in Boston  next week there's a whole week of  workshops about the letter folding and  this letters project anyway that but  what's so great about that is them you  can really see how people have handled  this paper and since this collection was  never handled very much you see that  there are sort of little personal  signatures in the way people fold their  letters which become more visible  through well I'm afraid we're out of  time so sorry we can encourage everyone  to continue the conversations as we move  to the next phase of the program but  before we adjourn and before we thank  our panelists I want to note that we're  going to have some closing remarks from  Susan Gibbons and but if you'll please  join me in thanking our  so thank you everyone for coming and for  joining us today I hope what you heard  today particularly for the audience it  inspires you to think about your own  research to think about questions that  you might have and there are ways to  continue this conversation if you look  at the back of your program you'll see  information about Yale steam one of our  sponsors today and there's a website  there they do programs throughout the  year and those programs are an  opportunity for you to learn more also  on the back is information about our  digital humanities lab here in the  library and every tuesday from 2 to 3 30  there's coffee and there's a and open  office hours where you can come and sit  down with the staff ask questions and  learn more so it's a way for you to  engage and start your own inquiry if  you've been inspired by what's happened  today so before I turn it over to all of  you I wanted to just make sure that we  give a round of applause for Cathy  DeRosa who really put together this  wonderful  so we have a poster session for you in  our memorabilia room so we can continue  the conversations out there thank you  all very much
 

